<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs">

  <?include ComponentGuids.wxi ?>

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Remembering WHERE the previous version was installed.

       The service itself never needs this: at runtime the install folder is AppContext.BaseDirectory (the
       exe's own folder) and the data folder is Jenkins:Agent:DataDirectory read from the appsettings.json
       sitting beside it. That chain only works once you already hold the exe.

       An upgrade is upstream of it. Windows Installer has to decide where to PUT the exe before anything can
       be read back, and the package recorded nothing to decide with - so INSTALLFOLDER and DATAFOLDER both
       reset to their defaults on every upgrade. Two consequences, neither cosmetic:

         * A non-default install folder was silently relocated to Program Files. appsettings.json is written
           by a custom action and is NOT a tracked MSI file, so it stays behind at the old path - the new
           install has no config, UpgradeConfig has nothing to reconcile, and the service comes up with no
           URL and no secret.
         * A non-default DATAFOLDER meant the DataFolderAcl component created and ACL'd a stray
           %ProgramData%\JenkinsAsService, granting the service account write on a folder nothing uses. The
           real data folder kept working (the config still pointed at it), so nothing failed loudly; and
           uninstall's purge reads DataDirectory out of the config, so the stray outlived the product.

       Both locations are therefore recorded at install time and searched for at the start of the next one.
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <Fragment>

    <!-- ─── 1. Record. Written on every install, including the new product of a major upgrade, so the values
             track the package rather than going stale. Tracked as a normal component, so a genuine uninstall
             removes the key and a reinstall does not inherit a dead path.

             HKLM, never HKCU: the product is perMachine and its custom actions run as SYSTEM, so a per-user
             hive would be both wrong and unreadable from the account that needs it.

             Each architecture owns a named subkey - SOFTWARE\JenkinsAsService\x64 and ...\x86 - so WHICH key
             holds a path is itself the statement of which architecture is installed. That is the only way a
             later package can tell a same-arch upgrade from a cross-arch migration: the two share an
             UpgradeCode, and FindRelatedProducts cannot distinguish them. It needs no separate value to go
             stale, and it is legible to anyone poking at the machine with regedit.

             No Bitness attribute - the component inherits the package's, so the x64 package writes the native
             64-bit view and the x86 package writes WOW6432Node. The searches below therefore read each key in
             the matching view, and read both, which is what makes the cross-architecture case detectable. ─── -->
    <!-- A GUID PER ARCHITECTURE, unlike ServiceComponent, and the difference matters. Component GUIDs are
         what Windows Installer reference-counts, and a GUID must always name the same resource - but these
         two components write different keys in different registry views. Sharing one GUID would make a forced
         cross-arch migration install the new component (refcount 2) and then remove the old product's
         reference (back to 1), so the OLD architecture's key would never be deleted. Both keys would then
         exist, and the next package would see two architectures installed at once and block against a product
         that is no longer there. ServiceComponent can share a GUID precisely because both packages install
         the same file to the same path. -->
    <?if $(var.InstallerPlatform) = "x64" ?>
      <?define LocationComponentGuid = "F06D514A-43FA-4FAE-B1F8-4B7A050B19E3" ?>
    <?else?>
      <?define LocationComponentGuid = "F49566AD-A619-40DF-BE18-CA39E6ABA61D" ?>
    <?endif?>
    <Component Id="InstallLocationRegistry" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER"
               Guid="$(var.LocationComponentGuid)">
      <RegistryKey Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\JenkinsAsService\$(var.InstallerPlatform)">
        <RegistryValue Name="InstallPath" Type="string" Value="[INSTALLFOLDER]" KeyPath="yes" />
        <RegistryValue Name="DataPath" Type="string" Value="[DATAFOLDER]" />
      </RegistryKey>
    </Component>

    <!-- ─── 2. Search. Three sources per location, deliberately overlapping.

             The registry values are the primary source and are read in BOTH views, so an x64 package sees an
             x86 install and vice versa.

             ComponentSearch is the fallback: it resolves the directory of a component by GUID out of
             Windows Installer's own component registration, which is architecture-neutral and needs nothing
             this package wrote. It covers the case the registry cannot - a product installed by a version
             that predates this key, which has no InstallPath to find. Those installs also carry no recorded
             Platform, so a cross-architecture migration away from one cannot be detected; see the gate below.
             ─── -->
    <Property Id="JAS_INSTALLDIR_X64" Secure="yes">
      <RegistrySearch Id="SearchInstallPathX64" Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\JenkinsAsService\x64"
                      Name="InstallPath" Type="raw" Bitness="always64" />
    </Property>
    <Property Id="JAS_INSTALLDIR_X86" Secure="yes">
      <RegistrySearch Id="SearchInstallPathX86" Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\JenkinsAsService\x86"
                      Name="InstallPath" Type="raw" Bitness="always32" />
    </Property>
    <Property Id="JAS_INSTALLDIR_COMPONENT" Secure="yes">
      <ComponentSearch Id="SearchInstallByComponent"
                       Guid="$(var.JasServiceComponentGuid)" Type="directory" />
    </Property>

    <Property Id="JAS_DATADIR_X64" Secure="yes">
      <RegistrySearch Id="SearchDataPathX64" Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\JenkinsAsService\x64"
                      Name="DataPath" Type="raw" Bitness="always64" />
    </Property>
    <Property Id="JAS_DATADIR_X86" Secure="yes">
      <RegistrySearch Id="SearchDataPathX86" Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\JenkinsAsService\x86"
                      Name="DataPath" Type="raw" Bitness="always32" />
    </Property>
    <Property Id="JAS_DATADIR_COMPONENT" Secure="yes">
      <ComponentSearch Id="SearchDataByComponent"
                       Guid="$(var.JasDataFolderComponentGuid)" Type="directory" />
    </Property>

    <!-- ─── 3. Apply. SetProperty (a type-51 property-setting custom action) rather than SetDirectory, so the
             scheduling and the precedence are visible in one place instead of being implied.

             Sequence="both" is load-bearing, not shorthand: these have to run in the UI sequence (which
             resolves the paths the wizard displays) AND the execute sequence (which resolves where the install
             actually writes, and is all a silent install runs). Authored once here, WiX emits both rows, so
             the two cannot drift - a condition fixed in only one of two hand-mirrored tables would give a
             wizard install and a /quiet install different install folders, and nothing in CI compares them.
             The explicit Action name is what lets several of these target one property: the generated custom
             action is named after it, so without it the six below would collide on one id.

             Precedence is FIRST match wins, encoded by the `NOT <target>` clause every one of them carries:
             once a higher-priority action has set the property, every later action's condition is false.
             Order: the x64 key, then the x86 key, then the component registration. Only one of the two keys
             can exist at a time - the packages share an UpgradeCode and cannot coexist - so the order between
             them settles nothing in practice; it is fixed so the behaviour is stated rather than incidental.

             That same clause is what protects an explicit override. Before CostFinalize a directory property
             holds a value ONLY if it was passed on the command line - the Directory table has not been
             costed yet - so `NOT INSTALLFOLDER` reads exactly as "the operator did not pass INSTALLFOLDER=".
             An operator who names a path still gets it; the recovery only fills a vacuum.

             Everything here must run after AppSearch (where the values above are found) and before
             CostFinalize (where directory properties are resolved into real paths). In this package that is
             AppSearch at 50, these at 51-60, CostFinalize at 1000. Both sequences are authored: the UI
             sequence needs the recovered paths for what it displays, the execute sequence for where it
             actually writes. ─── -->
    <!-- The x64 install folder is recovered ONLY by the x64 package, and that is not a tidiness choice.
         A 32-bit package cannot read a 64-bit Program Files path back out of the registry: the search itself
         works, but Windows Installer's WIN64DUALFOLDERS substitution then rewrites the RESULT, mapping the
         `C:\Program Files\` prefix of any folder-shaped AppSearch value to `C:\Program Files (x86)\`. Observed
         in CI, in the verbose log, verbatim:

             WIN64DUALFOLDERS: 'C:\Program Files (x86)\' will substitute 17 characters in
             'C:\Program Files\Jenkins\' folder path.
             PROPERTY CHANGE: Adding JAS_INSTALLDIR_X64 property. Its value is 'C:\Program Files (x86)\Jenkins\'

         So the x86 package would "recover" a path the previous version never used, install beside the real
         one, and leave appsettings.json - with the only copy of the secret - behind at the original location.
         That is the exact failure this whole file exists to prevent, which is why the x86 package refuses the
         migration outright rather than recovering a value it cannot trust (see the gate below).

         DATAFOLDER is unaffected and is recovered by both: %ProgramData% is not part of a dual-folder pair,
         so nothing rewrites it. -->
    <?if $(var.InstallerPlatform) = "x64" ?>
      <SetProperty Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Action="RecoverInstallDirFromX64" Value="[JAS_INSTALLDIR_X64]"
                   After="AppSearch" Sequence="both"
                   Condition="JAS_INSTALLDIR_X64 AND NOT INSTALLFOLDER" />
      <?define InstallDirChainAnchor = "RecoverInstallDirFromX64" ?>
    <?else?>
      <?define InstallDirChainAnchor = "AppSearch" ?>
    <?endif?>
    <SetProperty Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Action="RecoverInstallDirFromX86" Value="[JAS_INSTALLDIR_X86]"
                 After="$(var.InstallDirChainAnchor)" Sequence="both"
                 Condition="JAS_INSTALLDIR_X86 AND NOT INSTALLFOLDER" />
    <SetProperty Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Action="RecoverInstallDirFromComponent" Value="[JAS_INSTALLDIR_COMPONENT]"
                 After="RecoverInstallDirFromX86" Sequence="both"
                 Condition="JAS_INSTALLDIR_COMPONENT AND NOT INSTALLFOLDER" />
    <SetProperty Id="DATAFOLDER" Action="RecoverDataDirFromX64" Value="[JAS_DATADIR_X64]"
                 After="RecoverInstallDirFromComponent" Sequence="both"
                 Condition="JAS_DATADIR_X64 AND NOT DATAFOLDER" />
    <SetProperty Id="DATAFOLDER" Action="RecoverDataDirFromX86" Value="[JAS_DATADIR_X86]"
                 After="RecoverDataDirFromX64" Sequence="both"
                 Condition="JAS_DATADIR_X86 AND NOT DATAFOLDER" />
    <SetProperty Id="DATAFOLDER" Action="RecoverDataDirFromComponent" Value="[JAS_DATADIR_COMPONENT]"
                 After="RecoverDataDirFromX86" Sequence="both"
                 Condition="JAS_DATADIR_COMPONENT AND NOT DATAFOLDER" />

    <!-- The architecture of whatever is already installed - which is simply the name of the key that had a
         path in it. Left unset when only the component search found the product: an install from a version
         that predates these keys states no architecture, and guessing one would be worse than admitting it
         (so a migration away from such an install is not detected, and not blocked). -->
    <SetProperty Id="JAS_INSTALLED_PLATFORM" Action="RecordInstalledPlatformX64" Value="x64"
                 After="RecoverDataDirFromComponent" Sequence="both"
                 Condition="JAS_INSTALLDIR_X64 AND NOT JAS_INSTALLED_PLATFORM" />
    <SetProperty Id="JAS_INSTALLED_PLATFORM" Action="RecordInstalledPlatformX86" Value="x86"
                 After="RecordInstalledPlatformX64" Sequence="both"
                 Condition="JAS_INSTALLDIR_X86 AND NOT JAS_INSTALLED_PLATFORM" />

    <!-- ─── 4. The cross-architecture gate, which is deliberately ASYMMETRIC.

             x64 and x86 share an UpgradeCode, so MajorUpgrade will happily replace one with the other and
             FindRelatedProducts cannot tell the difference. There is no undo: the old product is removed at
             InstallInitialize, before anything can be reconsidered.

             x86 install -> x64 package is SUPPORTED, opt-in via FORCE_UPGRADE=1. Windows Installer takes
             PROPERTIES rather than command-line switches, so the opt-in is spelled FORCE_UPGRADE=1 rather
             than as a dashed flag, and the requirement is identical in the wizard and in a silent install: a
             silent run has nobody to prompt, and a run that would prompt has no business being easier to get
             wrong. When it is forced, the recovery above has already pointed INSTALLFOLDER and DATAFOLDER at
             the existing locations, so the migrated install lands on top of the config and data it inherits.

             x64 install -> x86 package is REFUSED OUTRIGHT, and FORCE_UPGRADE does not override it. The 32-bit
             package cannot recover the previous install folder: WIN64DUALFOLDERS rewrites the recovered path
             to `C:\Program Files (x86)\...` (see the note above the recovery), so the migration would install
             beside the real one and strand appsettings.json - the only copy of the secret - at the old path.
             A migration that silently destroys the thing it promises to keep is worse than one that refuses,
             so the package refuses and says which one to run instead.

             Nothing is lost by this. The bundle never needs the refused direction: its policy is the machine's
             NATIVE architecture, so on a 64-bit machine it installs x64 (the supported direction), and on a
             32-bit machine no x64 install can exist. Going deliberately from a 64-bit to a 32-bit agent is an
             uninstall followed by a fresh install - which is honest, because the config would have to be
             re-created at the new location either way.

             A type-19 error action rather than a LaunchCondition: LaunchConditions is sequenced at 100, after
             AppSearch (50) but the gate has to sit next to the searches it depends on, and authoring it as an
             error action keeps the two adjacent instead of split across files. It lands at 60 - far ahead of
             InstallInitialize (1500) and RemoveExistingProducts (1501) - so a blocked run aborts before the
             installed product has been touched at all.
             NOT Installed keeps it off the uninstall and maintenance paths; during a major upgrade the
             incoming ProductCode is not installed yet, so the gate still applies there. ─── -->
    <!-- Kept under 255 characters: CustomAction.Target is capped there (ICE03), the same limit that forced
         WriteAdvanced1/2/3 apart. The full explanation lives in docs/installation.html; this has to fit. -->
    <?if $(var.InstallerPlatform) = "x64" ?>
      <CustomAction Id="BlockArchMigration"
                    Error="[ProductName] is installed as [JAS_INSTALLED_PLATFORM]; this is the x64 package. Switching architecture is not reversible. Re-run with FORCE_UPGRADE=1 to migrate (config, secret and data are kept), or use the [JAS_INSTALLED_PLATFORM] package." />
      <!-- The whole precondition in one condition, rather than a separate action setting a JAS_ARCH_MISMATCH
           flag for this one consumer to read: an architecture is recorded, it is not this package's, and the
           migration was not asked for. -->
      <!-- Only the override TERM differs per architecture, so that is all the define carries: XML escapes
           inside a <?define?> would arrive at the condition as literal "&lt;" text and fail ICE03. -->
      <?define ArchGateOverride = "AND NOT FORCE_UPGRADE " ?>
    <?else?>
      <CustomAction Id="BlockArchMigration"
                    Error="[ProductName] is installed as 64-bit. A 32-bit package cannot take over that install - Windows Installer rewrites the recorded path, which would strand your configuration and agent secret. Install the x64 package, or the .exe installer." />
      <!-- No FORCE_UPGRADE term: this direction is refused, not gated. Adding an override would only offer a
           way to reach the broken path on purpose. -->
      <?define ArchGateOverride = "" ?>
    <?endif?>

    <!-- An error action cannot be scheduled with Sequence="both" the way the SetPropertys above are, so this
         is the one thing still authored per sequence - two rows for one action, and the condition itself comes
         from a single define, so there is nothing left to keep in sync by hand. -->
    <InstallUISequence>
      <Custom Action="BlockArchMigration" After="RecordInstalledPlatformX86"
              Condition="JAS_INSTALLED_PLATFORM AND JAS_INSTALLED_PLATFORM &lt;&gt; &quot;$(var.InstallerPlatform)&quot; $(var.ArchGateOverride)AND NOT Installed" />
    </InstallUISequence>

    <InstallExecuteSequence>
      <Custom Action="BlockArchMigration" After="RecordInstalledPlatformX86"
              Condition="JAS_INSTALLED_PLATFORM AND JAS_INSTALLED_PLATFORM &lt;&gt; &quot;$(var.InstallerPlatform)&quot; $(var.ArchGateOverride)AND NOT Installed" />
    </InstallExecuteSequence>

  </Fragment>

</Wix>
